/server/conflicts.ts::recordReview` — the only write path to `resolutions` + the primary write to `audit_log`; override-value validation, empty-proposal rejection.\n3. Concurrent-PATCH race on `resolutions` — no optimistic lock exists; two simultaneous reviews both \"succeed,\" last-write-wins. Documented, not guarded.\n4. `lib/server/rulesets.ts::createRulesetVersion` — the `pg_advisory_xact_lock` race guard against duplicate version numbers is unverified.\n5. `lib/server/export.ts::csvCell` (duplicated in `audit/export/route.ts`) — CSV escaping for commas/quotes/newlines/unicode in user-facing export artifacts.\n6. `app/api/conflicts/route.ts` — the one mutating-adjacent route with no explicit `requireReviewer()` call; relies entirely on `proxy.ts` middleware, unverified.\n7. `POST /api/runs` engine-unreachable path — must mark the run `failed` + audit `run_failed`, not leave it stuck in `queued`.\n\nBoth agents produced full per-file breakdowns (untested surface, edge cases, error-handling gaps, integration gaps) with runnable pytest/Vitest skeletons for every finding — that's the bulk of the output above, organized file-by-file under each layer.\n\n**Practical next step**, given zero test infra exists: this needs `pyproject.toml` pytest config + `tests/` for the engine, and a Vitest config + `web/vitest.config.ts` for the web layer, before any of these skeletons can run. Want me to set up that scaffolding (config only, no library additions beyond what's likely already implied by `pytest` in `pyproject.toml`) so these skeletons are drop-in runnable?\n",
